EXT. FROZEN QUARRY LAKE - DUSK

Black ice. Gray sky. Snow drifting sideways.

Four figures on the surface, roped together, each separated by ten feet of slack.

LEILA RAHIMI (30s, point, goggles fogged) tests the ice with a pole. Behind her, WYATT PRUITT (50s, quiet, bad knee) hauls a sled. Then CORAZON "CORA" DELGADO (20s, hood up), carrying the radio. Last, a boy of twelve, ANTON (swallowed by an adult parka), stays close to the sled.

Leila stops. Raps the pole twice. The ice answers with a dull, hollow note.

LEILA
Left. Wider.

WYATT
Left is the long way.

LEILA
Left.

CORA
We could cut across.

LEILA
We could.

Nobody moves. Wyatt adjusts the sled strap. Anton looks at the horizon.

WYATT
I used to skate here as a kid. Every winter. We'd race from the north wall to the pumphouse. I always lost.

CORA
You lost?

WYATT
My brother won. He was faster. He was always faster.

CORA
Where is he now?

WYATT
Montana.

CORA
That's far.

WYATT
It's far enough.

LEILA
Cora, how's the radio?

CORA
Quiet.

LEILA
Try it.

CORA
It's been quiet all day.

LEILA
Try it.

Cora thumbs the radio. Static. Nothing else. She lowers it.

WYATT
My brother says the ice thins near the pumphouse. Where the old pipe runs.

LEILA
Which is where we're headed.

WYATT
Yes.

LEILA
So you think we should go left.

WYATT
I think we should go left.

CORA
Anton, you okay?

Anton nods, not looking at her. He tugs the rope at his waist, once, twice, as though checking that it is real.

LEILA
Wyatt, tie the sled shorter.

WYATT
It'll drag.

LEILA
Then it drags.

He kneels, wincing. Shortens the line. The ice creaks. Everyone looks down.

CORA
That was nothing.

LEILA
That was something.

A long crack, like a rifle shot, rolls across the lake. They stand rigid. Anton drops to his knees, flat. The others follow, spreading their weight.

Silence. Snow falls on four still bodies.
